= 1
{parent=2007/A}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=1}

An <orientable surface> admits local coordinate orientations that are consistent on overlaps. Equivalently a <triangulation> can be oriented so that adjacent faces induce opposite directions on their common edge. Use a polygon word to record its successive boundary edges, identifying equal letters in their indicated directions; a negative exponent reverses that edge direction.

Square models are **$P:abab$, $K:aba^{-1}b$, and $T:aba^{-1}b^{-1}$**. The first identifies antipodal boundary points of a disk, giving the <real projective plane>. Only the <torus> among these three is <orientable>: its paired sides traverse the boundary oppositely, whereas each other model has a same-direction pair. The projective model has two vertex classes, and the other two one, giving Euler characteristics one and zero respectively.

The other possibility is **the <sphere>**, for example the word $aa^{-1}bb^{-1}$ with three vertex classes. There are no others: a paired square has one face, two edges and between one and three vertex classes, so its <Euler characteristic> is $V-1\in\{0,1,2\}$. The <classification of compact surfaces> leaves precisely the <sphere>, <real projective plane>, <torus> and <Klein bottle> at those values, with the indicated orientabilities.

For connected sums, remove a disk from each <surface>, join the resulting boundary circles, and cut a joining arc to produce a single polygon. Its word concatenates the two original words. The <real projective plane> also has the bigon word $cc$, so
$$
\boxed{T\mathbin{\#}P:\ aba^{-1}b^{-1}cc,\qquad T\mathbin{\#}K:\ aba^{-1}b^{-1}cdc^{-1}d.}
$$
These use respectively six and eight sides, proving the requested hexagon and octagon constructions. Their Euler characteristics are $-1$ and $-2$, also obtained from $\chi(X\#Y)=\chi(X)+\chi(Y)-2$.

= 2
{parent=2007/A}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2}

For a nonconstant <holomorphic map> of degree $d$ between compact connected <Riemann surfaces>,
$$
\boxed{2g_X-2=d(2g_Y-2)+\sum_{p\in X}(e_p-1),}
$$
where the <analytic ramification index> $e_p$ is the exponent in local coordinates $w\circ f=z^{e_p}$ after coordinate changes. A <meromorphic function> maps its <poles> to infinity; the target coordinate $1/w$ is then holomorphic and vanishes at a <pole>, so this defines a <holomorphic map> to the <Riemann sphere>. A <pole> of order $m$ has local degree $m$.

Let $\Lambda$ be the <period lattice>. The induced function $\wp:T=\mathbb C/\Lambda\to\mathbb{CP}^1$ has just one <pole>, of order two, so its degree is two. The <Riemann-Hurwitz formula> gives total ramification four. The <pole> contributes one; every <analytic ramification index> is at most two, so there are exactly three further <ramification points>, each simple. Evenness of $\wp$, obtained by reindexing its lattice sum, identifies them with the three nonzero half-periods. Their values $e_1,e_2,e_3$ are distinct: two points of local degree two cannot lie in one degree-two fiber.

The functions $\wp'^2$ and $\prod_i(\wp-e_i)$ have double zeros at these same three points and a <pole> of order six at zero. Their ratio is a holomorphic nowhere-zero function on the compact <torus>, hence constant. From $\wp(z)=z^{-2}+O(z^2)$ and $\wp'(z)=-2z^{-3}+O(z)$ its value is four. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\wp'^2=4(\wp-e_1)(\wp-e_2)(\wp-e_3),\qquad e_i\ne e_j\ (i\ne j).}
$$
**The printed additional assertion that all $e_i$ are nonzero is false.** The <square-lattice Weierstrass function has a zero branch value>: for $\mathbb Z+i\mathbb Z$, reindexing gives $\wp(iz)=-\wp(z)$. At the half-period $h=(1+i)/2$, one has $ih=h-1$, so periodicity gives $\wp(h)=-\wp(h)$ and hence $\wp(h)=0$. Thus one branch value is zero, while the three remain distinct.

The <derivative> has a single triple <pole>, so **$\deg\wp'=3$**, and the <Riemann-Hurwitz formula> gives **total ramification $\sum(e_p-1)=6$**. The number of distinct <ramification points> requires a further distinction. The absent constant term in the <Laurent expansion> gives $e_1+e_2+e_3=0$, so write $\wp'^2=4\wp^3-g_2\wp-g_3$. Differentiation yields $\wp''=6\wp^2-g_2/2$. If $g_2\ne0$, each of the two values $\pm\sqrt{g_2/12}$ has two distinct preimages under $\wp$ and none is a branch value, since a common zero of the cubic and its <derivative> would be a repeated cubic root. Thus there are four simple finite <ramification points> of $\wp'$ and its triple <pole>, giving **five distinct points**. If $g_2=0$, the nonsingularity of the cubic forces $g_3\ne0$. The two simple zeros of $\wp$ make $\wp''$ vanish to order two, so each has <analytic ramification index> three for $\wp'$. Together with its triple <pole> there are **three distinct points**. This second case occurs for a hexagonal lattice: its sixth-root rotational symmetry forces the $z^2$ Laurent coefficient, and hence $g_2$, to vanish. The weighted count is six in both cases.

= 3
{parent=2007/A}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=3}

A <critical point> satisfies $df=0$; it is nondegenerate when the <Hessian> on its two-dimensional <tangent space> is nonsingular. For a smooth function with only nondegenerate <critical points> on a compact <surface>, the <Morse theory> formula is
$$
\boxed{\chi(X)=m_0-m_1+m_2,}
$$
where $m_i$ counts the <critical points> whose <Hessian> has $i$ negative eigenvalues.

For $f(x)=|x|^2$, differentiation in a tangent direction $v$ gives $df_x(v)=2x\cdot v$. This vanishes for all $v\in T_xX$ exactly when the position vector is perpendicular to that <tangent plane>, equivalently the <normal line> at $x$ contains the origin. This includes $x=0$, if it is on the <surface>. On a <torus> $\chi=0$, while a minimum and a maximum give $m_0,m_2\geq1$. Thus $m_1=m_0+m_2\geq2$ and the total is at least four. Hence **at least four <surface> normals pass through the origin** under the stated nondegeneracy assumption.

Write the generating circle as center $(a,R)$ and radius $r$, with $R>r>0$. A parametrization and <unit normal> are
$$
X(u,v)=(a+r\cos u,(R+r\sin u)\cos v,(R+r\sin u)\sin v),\quad n(u,v)=(\cos u,\sin u\cos v,\sin u\sin v).
$$
Take **$P=(a,R/2,0)$**. If $P-X=tn$, its first coordinate gives $(r+t)\cos u=0$. The alternative $t=-r$ would force the radial distance of $P$ from the axis to be $R$, contrary to $R/2$. Thus $\cos u=0$. The remaining equations and the nonzero radial distance of $P$ force $v=0$ or $\pi$. Each of the two choices of $u$ and two of $v$ works, giving exactly four distinct points. Take **$Q=(a,0,0)$** instead. All points on each of the circles $u=\pi/2$ and $u=3\pi/2$ have radial normals through $Q$, giving infinitely many.

= 4
{parent=2007/A}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=4}

Parametrize a nonconstant <hyperbolic geodesic> by unit speed. Its energy is $(\dot x^2+\dot y^2)/(2y^2)$, and the <Euler-Lagrange equation> in the <cyclic coordinate> $x$ gives $\dot x/y^2=c$. Unit speed gives $\dot x^2+\dot y^2=y^2$. If $c=0$ the curve is vertical. Otherwise, on either side of a possible highest point,
$$
\frac{dx}{dy}=\frac{\pm cy}{\sqrt{1-c^2y^2}},\qquad (x-a)^2+y^2=c^{-2}.
$$
The two branches join smoothly at the top. Conversely these curves with their unit-speed parametrizations satisfy the energy equations. Thus **the unparametrized complete <geodesics> are vertical half-lines and upper semicircles centered on the real axis**.

Let the <tractrix> point have positive height $r(\theta)$. Its tangent segment to the axis has length one and makes angle $\theta$, so $r=\sin\theta$. Since its slope is $\tan\theta$, $h'(\theta)=\cos^2\theta/\sin\theta$ for its axial coordinate. Rotating $X(\theta,v)=(h(\theta),\sin\theta\cos v,\sin\theta\sin v)$ gives orthogonal coordinate vectors with squared lengths $\cot^2\theta$ and $\sin^2\theta$. Hence the stated <first fundamental form> follows.

Now put **$x=v$, $y=\csc\theta$**. Direct substitution gives
$$
\frac{dx^2+dy^2}{y^2}=\sin^2\theta\,dv^2+\cot^2\theta\,d\theta^2.
$$
On the specified intervals this coordinate map is a <diffeomorphism> onto the stated rectangle, so is an <isometry> there and a <local isometry> on the <tractoid>.

Set $b=\csc\beta$, $a=\csc\alpha$. In the unwrapped coordinate, connect $(x_0,b)$ to $(x_0+2\pi,b)$ along the semicircle centered at $(x_0+\pi,0)$ of radius $\sqrt{b^2+\pi^2}$. Its height lies between $b$ and that radius. The hypothesis $a^2-b^2>\pi^2$ puts the whole arc in the closed height strip $b\leq y\leq a$. Projecting the angular coordinate modulo $2\pi$ therefore gives **a <geodesic> segment in the closed <tractoid> annulus which winds once and returns to its starting point**. Taking $x_0$ to represent any chosen angular position proves the assertion for every point of its $\theta=\beta$ boundary. The returned tangent need not equal the starting tangent, so this proves the claimed returning segment without asserting a periodically smooth <closed geodesic>.

\Image[2007-tractoid-return.png]
{title=A returning <geodesic> in an unwrapped <tractoid> annulus}

